From 6c6af82dd4d9ac5781240fd1e93c4a0ffe230cfc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jordan McCallum <apt-install-coffee@proton.me>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 16:06:19 +1000
Subject: [PATCH 150/150] reset: fall back to lookup table for non-OF firmware
 nodes

__reset_control_get() routes any device with a firmware node to
__fwnode_reset_control_get(), which requires a "reset-names" property
on the node.  ACPI devices have firmware nodes but describe reset
consumption via the lookup table mechanism instead, so the fwnode path
returns -ENOENT for them and the lookup table is never consulted.

Restore the pre-fwnode dispatch semantics: only device-tree nodes use
the fwnode path; other consumers resolve through the lookup table.
This restores ACPI reset consumption for the Sky1 platform, where the
vendor DSDT provides RSTL tables consumed by the CIXA1019
resource-lookup driver.
---
 drivers/reset/core.c | 8 +++++++-
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/reset/core.c b/drivers/reset/core.c
index 8ecdf3bb5c19..b5ee23595872 100644
--- a/drivers/reset/core.c
+++ b/drivers/reset/core.c
@@ -1335,7 +1335,13 @@ struct reset_control *__reset_control_get(struct device *dev, const char *id,
 	if (WARN_ON(shared && acquired))
 		return ERR_PTR(-EINVAL);
 
-	if (fwnode)
+	/*
+	 * Device-tree nodes describe resets via firmware properties; ACPI
+	 * and other non-OF firmware nodes have no equivalent, so their
+	 * consumers resolve through the lookup table registered by the
+	 * provider driver, as under the pre-fwnode API.
+	 */
+	if (fwnode && is_of_node(fwnode))
 		return __fwnode_reset_control_get(fwnode, id, index, flags);
 
 	return __reset_control_get_from_lookup(dev, id, flags);
